  "id": "scapular-elevation",
  "name": "Scapular Elevation",
  "definition": "A strict open-chain scapular isolation whose concentric phase elevates the scapulae while humeral and elbow angles remain fixed. The contract admits the directly measured single-arm dumbbell shoulder shrug and Lee et al.'s exact bilateral stabilization shrug at 30 degrees of frontal-plane abduction. Both model the observed upward rotation as a coupled prime action instead of pretending that a shrug is pure vertical translation: levator scapulae and upper trapezius elevate the scapula, while upper trapezius, serratus, and in the 30-degree fixture lower trapezius contribute to upward rotation. The dumbbell trace showed smaller model-specific abduction/adduction and winging coordinates; those remain disclosed coupling rather than incorrectly relabeled catalog actions. The stabilization fixture uses no external training load: two source trials, a five-second hold at the individual maximum-height target bars, light radial-wrist contact with frontal guides, inclinometer-maintained abduction, and investigator stabilization of the chin and thorax are pinned as laboratory topology. Lee et al. measured scapular upward-rotation angle immediately after each exercise condition rather than continuously through the shrug, so the coupled action is a condition-level finding rather than a dynamic angle trace. Rolling shrugs, overhead shrugs, trap-bar and barbell variants, machine shrugs, carries, upright rows, unsupported 30-degree shrugs, neck motion, and leg-driven pulls remain outside this narrow contract.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "castelein-2016-scapular-muscles-shrug",
    "lee-2016-stabilization-shrug-upward-rotation",
    "seth-2019-shoulder-work",
    "werthel-2019-trapezius-transfer"
  ],
